---
import CaseStudyLayout from "../../layouts/CaseStudyLayout.astro";
import BeforeAfter from "../../components/BeforeAfter.astro";
---

<CaseStudyLayout
	slug="datashare"
	title="DataShare Santa Cruz County"
	subtitle="Platform Redesign"
	tags={["UX Research", "Information Architecture", "Usability Testing"]}
	summary="Redesigned Santa Cruz County's public data hub into a clearer, community-centered experience for finding, interpreting, and acting on public health data."
	role="Content Strategy, UX Research, UX Design"
	timeline="April–October 2025 (7 months)"
	tools={["Figma", "HTML", "Google Analytics"]}
	skills={[
		"User Research",
		"Usability Testing",
		"Qualitative Analysis",
		"Information Architecture",
		"Wireframing",
		"Prototyping",
	]}
>
	<Fragment slot="visual">
		<img
			src="/images/case-studies/datashare/macbook-mockup.png"
			alt="The redesigned DataShare Santa Cruz County homepage, shown on a laptop screen"
			width={1400}
			height={900}
		/>
	</Fragment>

	<Fragment slot="overview">
		<p>
			DataShare Santa Cruz County is the county's public data hub — a platform that
			centralizes health, demographic, and community indicators for residents,
			nonprofits, educators, and policymakers. It matters because access to this data
			shapes real decisions: where resources go, which communities get support, and
			whether people can advocate for their own neighborhoods with real evidence behind
			them. My goal was to redesign the platform into a clearer, more intuitive,
			community-centered experience — one that helps users find, interpret, and act on
			public health data with confidence.
		</p>
	</Fragment>

	<Fragment slot="problem">
		<p>
			The original site contained valuable information but lacked clarity and structure.
			Users struggled to locate indicators, navigate redundant pathways, and interpret
			charts. Many took multiple routes to reach the same page — a clear sign the
			information architecture didn't match how people actually think about the content.
		</p>
		<BeforeAfter
			beforeSrc="/images/case-studies/datashare/original-full.jpg"
			beforeAlt="The original DataShare homepage, full page"
			afterSrc="/images/case-studies/datashare/staging-full.jpg"
			afterAlt="The redesigned DataShare homepage on staging, full page"
		/>
	</Fragment>

	<Fragment slot="process">
		<details class="phase">
			<summary>Discover</summary>
			<div class="phase-body">
				<p>
					I audited the existing site and analyzed peer public-data platforms. This
					surfaced overly technical terminology, dropdown menus that didn't reflect user
					mental models, and a dense, text-heavy layout that overwhelmed new users.
				</p>
			</div>
		</details>
		<details class="phase">
			<summary>Design</summary>
			<div class="phase-body">
				<p>
					I restructured the site map around real community use cases instead of internal
					terminology, simplifying labels and reducing menu depth. Low-fidelity wireframes
					let me test navigation quickly; high-fidelity designs then introduced a
					redesigned homepage with a Monthly Focus tile, consolidated dropdowns, and
					clearer dashboard layouts.
				</p>
				<figure>
					<img
						src="/images/case-studies/datashare/new-dropdowns.gif"
						alt="Demo of the redesigned DataShare navigation with consolidated dropdown menus"
					/>
					<figcaption>The redesigned navigation, consolidated into five goal-oriented categories</figcaption>
				</figure>
			</div>
		</details>
		<details class="phase">
			<summary>Test</summary>
			<div class="phase-body">
				<p>
					I ran moderated usability sessions with 10 participants — 6 returning users and
					4 new users — comparing the original and redesigned site through think-aloud
					tasks like finding demographic data or locating community initiatives.
				</p>
			</div>
		</details>
	</Fragment>

	<Fragment slot="decisions">
		<ul>
			<li>
				Navigation labels didn't match how people think — nested dropdowns using internal
				terms like "Coalitions" confused users.
				<blockquote>"I'm gonna be honest, I don't really know what coalitions mean."</blockquote>
				 In response, I simplified labels and reduced menu depth around real community use
				cases instead of internal terminology.
			</li>
			<li>
				Accessibility gaps excluded real users — several indicators failed WCAG
				color-contrast standards.
				<blockquote>
					"Is this whole bar here green? Or is the separation of colors? I can't see the
					difference there, actually, at all."
				</blockquote>
				 I revised the color system before the design shipped to staging.
			</li>
			<li>
				Simplifying an interface can remove functionality people depend on — returning
				users had workflows, like the Data Spotlight feature and custom dashboard links,
				that the initial redesign broke. I restored these shortcuts in clearer locations
				rather than cutting them for the sake of simplicity.
			</li>
		</ul>
	</Fragment>

	<Fragment slot="outcome">
		<p>
			7 of 10 participants preferred the redesigned site — 5 of 6 returning users and 2 of
			4 new users. I used these findings to revise the final design, restoring key
			navigation shortcuts, fixing accessibility issues, and clarifying labels, before it
			shipped to the staging site.
		</p>
	</Fragment>

	<Fragment slot="reflection">
		<p>
			This project taught me that clean visuals don't guarantee usability, and that even
			well-intentioned simplification can create new friction if it isn't grounded in real
			user behavior. It reinforced my commitment to designing with users, not just for
			them — and sharpened how I translate qualitative research into concrete design
			decisions.
		</p>
	</Fragment>

	<Fragment slot="whatsNext">
		<ul>
			<li>Refine search to return broader, more predictable results.</li>
			<li>Reintroduce key features — Data Spotlight, custom dashboards — in clearer locations.</li>
			<li>Run a second round of testing with color-blind and low-vision users.</li>
			<li>Explore a lightweight onboarding flow for new users.</li>
		</ul>
	</Fragment>
</CaseStudyLayout>
